=====================================================================
lisptc — memory: remembering, firing on events, chaining, forgetting

Run me with the interactive REPL:
  pnpm repl
which wires the memory extension with a file-backed store, so what
you remember here is still here next time. Point it somewhere else
with LISPTC_MEMORY_DIR, or wipe it by deleting that directory.

Built-ins, installed by the memory extension — src/memory.ts:
  memory/remember  — store a memory under a key
  memory/recall    — retrieve by key or body, strongest first
  memories         — list every memory with its strength
  memory/forget    — drop one
  memory/replay    — evaluate a memory whose body is a form
  memory/revise    — correct one, inside the window retrieval opens
=====================================================================
(echo "=== 1. a declarative memory: something to know ===")

The body is prose. This is the half of memory that loads text into
your context.
(memory/remember "widget-ids" "Widget ids are short strings, never numbers.")
=> "widget-ids"

(echo (memory/recall "widget"))
=> one memory, as an alist of key, body, score and used

(echo "=== 2. a procedural memory: something to do ===")

The body is a QUOTED form. Without the quote you would store the
result of evaluating it, not the recipe.
(memory/remember "double" '(defun double (x) (* x 2)))
=> "double"

double is not defined yet — the memory holds the form, nothing ran.
(memory/replay "double")
(echo (double 21))
=> 42

That is the homoiconic part: prose and code live in the same slot
because in this language they are the same material.

(echo "=== 3. firing on an event ===")

With :on the memory watches for something and loads itself when it
happens. For a call the pattern is a FORM, matched structurally, so
naming no arguments hooks every call to that name.
(memory/remember "joins" "string-join takes the list FIRST, then the separator."
                 :on '(call (string-join)))

Now write a form that calls it, and the note arrives with the result
of the step — before you get the argument order wrong.
(echo (string-join '("a" "b") ","))

Name an argument and the memory gets precise: this one only fires for
the widget tool, not for every call to concat.
(memory/remember "widget-concat" "widget ids concatenate with a dash, never a slash."
                 :on '(call (concat "widget" _)))
(echo (concat "widget" "-1a2b"))

A bare _ matches one argument whatever it is, a head is a regular
expression, and all, any-of and not combine patterns.

The seven kinds are call, result, error, step, prose, user and
recall. An error trigger is the useful one: a mistake you made once
surfaces exactly as it is about to repeat.
(memory/remember "undefineds" "Check (dump) before calling a name you have not defined."
                 :on '(error "undefined"))

(echo "=== 4. chaining ===")

Retrieval is itself an event, so one memory can hook another. Recall
the first and both arrive.
(memory/remember "widget-shape" "A widget alist has id, name and state."
                 :on '(recall "widget-ids"))

You can also say it outright with :links. And memories that fire in
the same step get wired to each other on their own, so what you use
together starts arriving together.

(echo "=== 5. strength, and forgetting ===")

Every memory has a score. Recalling strengthens it; silence lets it
fade, half its strength a week, and under a floor it is swept away.
(echo (memories))
=> each memory as key, score and trigger, strongest first

(memory/forget "joins")
=> t, and it is gone for good

(echo "=== 6. the window ===")

Retrieving a memory opens it for correction until the step ends.
Inside that window revise works:
(memory/recall "widget-ids")
(memory/revise "widget-ids" "Widget ids look like w_1a2b — short strings, never numbers.")

Outside it, revise is an error telling you to recall it first. Reading
and rewriting are one act on purpose: you fix a memory while you are
looking at it and can see that it was wrong.
